istance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{1}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{1}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{1}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{1}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{1} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]‖}_{1} \\\\  & =|3-4|+|5-(-1)|+|1-6| \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1db7e5dd-95fa-58c9-add2-98949ac75558"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1db7e5dd-95fa-58c9-add2-98949ac75558" :answer-field/id #uuid "1db7e5dd-95fa-58c9-add2-98949ac75558" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-eeaef794-0de7-5ce7-8a12-9497ddf3853d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eeaef794-0de7-5ce7-8a12-9497ddf3853d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eeaef794-0de7-5ce7-8a12-9497ddf3853d" :answer/id #uuid "eeaef794-0de7-5ce7-8a12-9497ddf3853d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f3e294b9-f845-51ae-87f3-5926acf855f0" :question/id #uuid "f3e294b9-f845-51ae-87f3-5926acf855f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-254857" :question/problem "Suppose $a=[7,\\,4]^{T}$ and $b=[3,\\,-2]^{T}.$ Find the Manhattan distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{1}(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Manhattan distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{1}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{1}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{1}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{1}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{1} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]‖}_{1} \\\\  & =|7-3|+|4-(-2)| \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d8544c82-98cb-5137-bc05-8e0dd6402c7f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d8544c82-98cb-5137-bc05-8e0dd6402c7f" :answer-field/id #uuid "d8544c82-98cb-5137-bc05-8e0dd6402c7f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a42d1223-744c-5351-b0e1-ced50d043bb0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a42d1223-744c-5351-b0e1-ced50d043bb0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a42d1223-744c-5351-b0e1-ced50d043bb0" :answer/id #uuid "a42d1223-744c-5351-b0e1-ced50d043bb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-83af6a91-b190-50f3-bc76-025dc5091d7d" :question/id #uuid "83af6a91-b190-50f3-bc76-025dc5091d7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-254864" :question/problem "Suppose $a=[1,\\,4,\\,-2]^{T}$ and $b=[-4,\\,-1,\\,-2]^{T}.$ Find the Manhattan distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{1}(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Manhattan distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{1}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{1}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{1}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{1}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{1} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]‖}_{1} \\\\  & =|1-(-4)|+|4-(-1)|+|-2-(-2)| \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cdfb46aa-95f2-5e0c-93b7-e108a22585c2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cdfb46aa-95f2-5e0c-93b7-e108a22585c2" :answer-field/id #uuid "cdfb46aa-95f2-5e0c-93b7-e108a22585c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-17fbba71-b65d-5cc9-9d93-3fa361546a64" :answer-field/choices ["answer-17fbba71-b65d-5cc9-9d93-3fa361546a64"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17fbba71-b65d-5cc9-9d93-3fa361546a64" :answer/id #uuid "17fbba71-b65d-5cc9-9d93-3fa361546a64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157710 :question/problem "Suppose $a=[1,\\,-1]^{T},$ and $b=[-2,\\,1]^{T},$ and $p=3.$ Find the Minkowski distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\nRounded to three decimal places, ${d}_{p}(a,b)≈$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Minkowski distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{p}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{p}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{p}={({\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{p})}^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{3}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{3} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]‖}_{3} \\\\  & ={(|1-(-2){|}^{3}+|-1-1{|}^{3})}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={35}^{1/3} \\\\  & ≈3.271.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186154800 :answer-field/correct "answer-ae0de384-07cc-5a84-afc1-a9358cfadaec" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ae0de384-07cc-5a84-afc1-a9358cfadaec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae0de384-07cc-5a84-afc1-a9358cfadaec" :answer/id #uuid "ae0de384-07cc-5a84-afc1-a9358cfadaec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.271" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cd8b6d86-5eba-5e13-9d83-5bea3e076943" :question/id #uuid "cd8b6d86-5eba-5e13-9d83-5bea3e076943" :question/math-academy-id "q-254889" :question/problem "Suppose $a=[3,\\,1]^{T},$ and $b=[2,\\,2]^{T},$ and $p=5.$ Find the Minkowski distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\nRounded to three decimal places, ${d}_{p}(a,b)≈$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Minkowski distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{p}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{p}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{p}={({\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{p})}^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{5}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{5} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]‖}_{5} \\\\  & ={(|3-2{|}^{5}+|1-2{|}^{5})}^{1/5} \\\\  & ={2}^{1/5} \\\\  & ≈1.149.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5abe70ca-7e0c-590c-bb75-0dcc742b5859"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5abe70ca-7e0c-590c-bb75-0dcc742b5859" :answer-field/id #uuid "5abe70ca-7e0c-590c-bb75-0dcc742b5859" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-92424c36-a24d-582d-9308-f8f12010911d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-92424c36-a24d-582d-9308-f8f12010911d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92424c36-a24d-582d-9308-f8f12010911d" :answer/id #uuid "92424c36-a24d-582d-9308-f8f12010911d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.149" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2ca9d6e1-42a8-5184-8cd9-770775f3f1b2" :question/id #uuid "2ca9d6e1-42a8-5184-8cd9-770775f3f1b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-254902" :question/problem "Suppose $a=[5,\\,-3,\\,4]^{T},$ and $b=[4,\\,-1,\\,2]^{T},$ and $p=4.$ Find the Minkowski distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\nRounded to three decimal places, ${d}_{p}(a,b)≈$ \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Minkowski distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{p}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{p}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{p}={({\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{p})}^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{4}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{4} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]‖}_{4} \\\\  & ={(|5-4{|}^{4}+|-3-(-1){|}^{4}+|4-2{|}^{4})}^{1/4} \\\\  & ={33}^{1/4} \\\\  & ≈2.397.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9aa93ebe-bf5f-5031-a273-47db623e3948"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9aa93ebe-bf5f-5031-a273-47db623e3948" :answer-field/id #uuid "9aa93ebe-bf5f-5031-a273-47db623e3948" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-bd4c9d49-3a7a-57e3-8d57-5591fee3c393" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bd4c9d49-3a7a-57e3-8d57-5591fee3c393"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd4c9d49-3a7a-57e3-8d57-5591fee3c393" :answer/id #uuid "bd4c9d49-3a7a-57e3-8d57-5591fee3c393" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.397" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ae95185c-810c-50b4-b278-d16c314036aa" :question/id #uuid "ae95185c-810c-50b4-b278-d16c314036aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-254904" :question/problem "Suppose $a=[1,\\,1,\\,-1]^{T},$ and $b=[2,\\,-1,\\,-2]^{T},$ and $p=3.$ Find the Minkowski distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\nRounded to three decimal places, ${d}_{p}(a,b)≈$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Minkowski distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{p}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{p}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{p}={({\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{p})}^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{3}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{3} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]‖}_{3} \\\\  & ={(|1-2{|}^{3}+|1-(-1){|}^{3}+|-1-(-2){|}^{3})}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={10}^{1/3} \\\\  & ≈2.154.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4646076f-348f-5d4b-bcaf-606d564e3120"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4646076f-348f-5d4b-bcaf-606d564e3120" :answer-field/id #uuid "4646076f-348f-5d4b-bcaf-606d564e3120" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6b6d080b-addf-5469-8035-84ef6b8b351b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6b6d080b-addf-5469-8035-84ef6b8b351b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b6d080b-addf-5469-8035-84ef6b8b351b" :answer/id #uuid "6b6d080b-addf-5469-8035-84ef6b8b351b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.154" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f4c50ce1-0092-5528-9c12-822dbb56b8d9" :question/id #uuid "f4c50ce1-0092-5528-9c12-822dbb56b8d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-254905" :question/problem "Suppose $a=[2,\\,-1,\\,1]^{T},$ and $b=[3,\\,0,\\,2]^{T},$ and $p=6.$ Find the Minkowski distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\nRounded to three decimal places, ${d}_{p}(a,b)≈$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Minkowski distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{p}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{p}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{p}={({\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{p})}^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{6}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{6} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]‖}_{6} \\\\  & ={(|2-3{|}^{6}+|-1-0{|}^{6}+|1-2{|}^{6})}^{1/6} \\\\  & ={3}^{1/6} \\\\  & ≈1.201.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aecf6ce9-a1f4-555e-9702-8cc70bee1442"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aecf6ce9-a1f4-555e-9702-8cc70bee1442" :answer-field/id #uuid "aecf6ce9-a1f4-555e-9702-8cc70bee1442" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-cf04d7d1-03ee-5067-9205-1285cffc22a4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cf04d7d1-03ee-5067-9205-1285cffc22a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf04d7d1-03ee-5067-9205-1285cffc22a4" :answer/id #uuid "cf04d7d1-03ee-5067-9205-1285cffc22a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.201" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157707 :question/problem "Suppose $a=[8,\\,4,\\,-3]^{T}$ and $b=[-4,\\,4,\\,-1]^{T}.$ Find the Chebyshev distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|8-(-4)|,|4-4|,|-3-(-1)|) \\\\  & =max(12,0,2) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186154797 :answer-field/correct "answer-9431c376-6b65-5c1c-abe5-8d53adefe289" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9431c376-6b65-5c1c-abe5-8d53adefe289"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9431c376-6b65-5c1c-abe5-8d53adefe289" :answer/id #uuid "9431c376-6b65-5c1c-abe5-8d53adefe289" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-78d962fb-98a1-54a9-be40-612e78bd3a7f" :question/id #uuid "78d962fb-98a1-54a9-be40-612e78bd3a7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-254909" :question/problem "Suppose $a=[7,\\,6,\\,-2]^{T}$ and $b=[-1,\\,2,\\,4]^{T}.$ Find the Chebyshev distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|7-(-1)|,|6-2|,|-2-4|) \\\\  & =max(8,4,6) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-57eb417a-dd62-519e-be44-c8803a43e66f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-57eb417a-dd62-519e-be44-c8803a43e66f" :answer-field/id #uuid "57eb417a-dd62-519e-be44-c8803a43e66f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-006ced18-63ca-5194-b549-3abf13fc8a1f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-006ced18-63ca-5194-b549-3abf13fc8a1f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-006ced18-63ca-5194-b549-3abf13fc8a1f" :answer/id #uuid "006ced18-63ca-5194-b549-3abf13fc8a1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e36e927d-aa55-5840-81c8-aa7b916f3c2f" :question/id #uuid "e36e927d-aa55-5840-81c8-aa7b916f3c2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-254913" :question/problem "Suppose $a=[5,\\,3]^{T}$ and $b=[9,\\,-9]^{T}.$ Find the Chebyshev distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|5-9|,|3-(-9)|) \\\\  & =max(4,12) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3d4c5933-33d1-59b9-ac6d-fe94b7f8b82a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3d4c5933-33d1-59b9-ac6d-fe94b7f8b82a" :answer-field/id #uuid "3d4c5933-33d1-59b9-ac6d-fe94b7f8b82a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4444bb67-364a-5f5c-8706-f6a58ebeff4b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4444bb67-364a-5f5c-8706-f6a58ebeff4b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4444bb67-364a-5f5c-8706-f6a58ebeff4b" :answer/id #uuid "4444bb67-364a-5f5c-8706-f6a58ebeff4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-79a48270-e1b9-5009-9c26-f6b63d86b07c" :question/id #uuid "79a48270-e1b9-5009-9c26-f6b63d86b07c" :question/math-academy-id "q-254915" :question/problem "Suppose $a=[0,\\,-6]^{T}$ and $b=[0.5,\\,-7]^{T}.$ Find the Chebyshev distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|0-0.5|,|-6-(-7)|) \\\\  & =max(0.5,1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5175a214-a64f-5932-be81-c533d3570807"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5175a214-a64f-5932-be81-c533d3570807" :answer-field/id #uuid "5175a214-a64f-5932-be81-c533d3570807" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2c6e500d-6110-570c-ab83-90ca0a6cd642" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2c6e500d-6110-570c-ab83-90ca0a6cd642"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c6e500d-6110-570c-ab83-90ca0a6cd642" :answer/id #uuid "2c6e500d-6110-570c-ab83-90ca0a6cd642" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7ac6fcb3-d9b4-5799-8b69-524dd3ce6133" :question/id #uuid "7ac6fcb3-d9b4-5799-8b69-524dd3ce6133" :question/math-academy-id "q-254910" :question/problem "Suppose $a=[-3,\\,5,\\,1]^{T}$ and $b=[2,\\,7,\\,4]^{T}.$ Find the Chebyshev distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|-3-2|,|5-7|,|1-4|) \\\\  & =max(5,2,3) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-132b1cae-24b3-5188-adb0-73906416cf99"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-132b1cae-24b3-5188-adb0-73906416cf99" :answer-field/id #uuid "132b1cae-24b3-5188-adb0-73906416cf99" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d2727e3c-066f-56f4-a2a7-2b6b842a164d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d2727e3c-066f-56f4-a2a7-2b6b842a164d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2727e3c-066f-56f4-a2a7-2b6b842a164d" :answer/id #uuid "d2727e3c-066f-56f4-a2a7-2b6b842a164d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158439 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we’ll explore different ways to measure how far apart two points are in space.\n\nTo formally define a distance between two points $x$ and $y$, we use a **metric function** or **distance function**. This is a rule $d(x, y)$ that assigns a non-negative number to each pair of points, representing how far apart they are.\n\nA valid distance function must satisfy these four properties:\n\n- *Identity*:\n\n$$\nd(x, x) = 0\n$$\n\n- *Positivity*:\n\n$$\nd(x, y) > 0 \\quad \\text{if} \\quad x \\ne y\n$$\n\n- *Symmetry*:\n\n$$\nd(x, y) = d(y, x)\n$$\n\n- *Triangle inequality*:\n\n$$\nd(x, z) \\leq d(x, y) + d(y, z)\n$$\n\nThese conditions ensure the function behaves like a meaningful measure of distance.\n\nLet’s consider some widely used concrete examples of metrics." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158464 :tutorial/content "The most familiar example of a metric is the **Euclidean distance**, which represents the straight-line distance between two points. \n\nFor two points $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$, it is defined as\n\n$$\nd_2(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) = \\sqrt{ \\sum_{i=1}^N (x_i - y_i)^2 }.\n$$\n\nEuclidean distance measures the shortest path directly connecting the points, as shown in the figure below.\n\n![](images/a9/a9d42944e5bca8beb26cbc9ed76ef5de5ee72d60319c5077cc472170167eb9e0.png)\n\nNext, we will look at an alternative metric based on grid-like movement." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158465 :tutorial/content "Another common metric is the **Manhattan distance**, also known as **taxicab distance**. \n\nIt is defined as\n\n$$\nd_1(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) = \\sum_{i=1}^N |x_i - y_i|.\n$$\n\nManhattan distance measures the total movement along a grid, adding the distance traveled in each coordinate direction rather than cutting across the diagonal. This is shown in the figure below.\n\n![](images/97/97c57d903af41281bc84acb103cb4f8def01dd8a1de5671a32107f58b93f177d.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158458 :tutorial/content "Although Manhattan and Euclidean distances are defined in different ways, they have something important in common. What matters for both is the difference between the two points, not their absolute positions. \n\nEach one measures the length of the vector connecting the points, just with a different notion of distance. This makes it possible to express both using the idea of a norm.\n\nA **norm** is a function that measures the size or length of a vector. \n\nThere are many types of norms, but two of the most commonly used, and closely related to the distance metrics we just defined, are the **Euclidean norm** and the **Manhattan norm**.\n\n- The **Euclidean norm** (also called the $L^2$ norm) is given by\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v} \\|_2 = \\sqrt{ \\sum_{i=1}^N v_i^2 }.\n$$\n\nThis is the usual length of a vector in geometric terms.\n\n- The **Manhattan norm** (also called the $L^1$ norm) is given by\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v} \\|_1 = \\sum_{i=1}^N |v_i|.\n$$\n\nThis gives the total amount of movement in all coordinate directions.\n\nNow we can rewrite our earlier distance formulas using norms. The Euclidean distance becomes\n\n$$\nd_2(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) = \\| \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{y} \\|_2,\n$$\n\nand the Manhattan distance becomes\n\n$$\nd_1(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) = \\| \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{y} \\|_1.\n$$\n\nThis makes the connection between distances and vector norms clear; distances are just norms of differences.\n\nNow, let's work through a few examples to understand how to compute distances step by step." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158440 :tutorial/content "We’ve seen how both Euclidean and Manhattan distances can be written in terms of vector norms. These are actually special cases of a more general formula known as the **Minkowski distance**.\n\nGiven a fixed value $p \\ge 1$, the Minkowski distance between two points $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ in $N$-dimensional space is defined as\n\n$$\nd_p(\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}) = \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^N |a_i - b_i|^p \\right)^{\\frac{1}{p}}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding **Minkowski norm** is given by\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v} \\|_p = \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^N |v_i|^p \\right)^{\\frac{1}{p}}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $p$ determines how differences in individual coordinates contribute to the total distance:\n\n- **$p=1$** (Manhattan): All differences are summed equally.\n\n- **$p=2$** (Euclidean): Larger differences have more weight due to squaring.\n\n- **$p \\to \\infty$**: The distance is determined entirely by the single largest coordinate difference.\n\nLet’s compute an example to see how this works in practice.\n\nSuppose $\\mathbf{a} = [1,\\ 2,\\ 3]^T$ and $\\mathbf{b} = [4,\\ 0,\\ 6]^T$. Let's compute their Minkowski distance using $p = 5$.\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{5}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{5} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]‖}_{5} \\\\  & ={(|1-4{|}^{5}+|2-0{|}^{5}+|3-6{|}^{5})}^{1/5} \\\\  & ={(|-3{|}^{5}+{2}^{5}+|-3{|}^{5})}^{1/5} \\\\  & ={(243+32+243)}^{1/5} \\\\  & ={518}^{1/5} \\\\  & ≈3.490.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's try a few examples to build intuition." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158441 :tutorial/content "Now that we’ve learned how the Minkowski norm depends on the parameter $p$, let’s see what happens when we keep increasing $p$.\n\nRecall that the Minkowski norm of a vector $\\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^N$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v} \\|_p = \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^N |v_i|^p \\right)^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nLet’s explore how this behaves using a concrete example. Consider the vector $\\mathbf{v} = [1,\\ 2,\\ 3]^T$.\n\nNow compute its Minkowski norms for increasing values of $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v{‖}_{2} & ={({1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2})}^{1/2}=(14)^{1/2}≈3.742 \\\\ ‖v{‖}_{5} & ={({1}^{5}+{2}^{5}+{3}^{5})}^{1/5}={276}^{1/5}≈3.077 \\\\ ‖v{‖}_{10} & ={({1}^{10}+{2}^{10}+{3}^{10})}^{1/10}={60074}^{1/10}≈3.005 \\\\ ‖v{‖}_{100} & ={({1}^{100}+{2}^{100}+{3}^{100})}^{1/100}≈3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $p$ increases, the smaller components contribute less and less to the overall value. The norm becomes increasingly influenced by the largest coordinate, in this case, $3.$\n\nLet’s now understand why this happens more formally. Suppose $\\mathbf{v}$ is a nonzero vector, and that one of its entries is strictly larger than all the others. Let $j$ be the index where $|v_j| > |v_i|$ for every $i \\ne j$.\n\nOur goal is to understand the behavior of the norm as $p \\to \\infty{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{p \\to \\infty} \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|_p = \\lim_{p \\to \\infty} \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^N |v_i|^p \\right)^{1/p}\n$$\n\nTo simplify this expression, we factor out $|v_j|^p$ from the sum inside\n\n$$\n\\lim_{p \\to \\infty} \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|_p = \\lim_{p \\to \\infty} |v_j| \\left(1 + \\sum_{i \\ne j} \\left( \\frac{|v_i|}{|v_j|} \\right)^p \\right)^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nSince $|v_j|$ is the largest component, each ratio $\\dfrac{|v_i|}{|v_j|}$ is a number less than $1.$ When we raise numbers less than $1$ to larger and larger powers, they shrink toward zero.\n\nThat leaves us with\n\n$$\n\\lim_{p \\to \\infty} \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|_p = |v_j| \\cdot 1 = |v_j|.\n$$\n\nThis motivates the definition of the **Chebyshev norm** (also called the **max norm** or $L^\\infty$ norm) as\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|_\\infty = \\max\\left( |v_1|,\\ |v_2|,\\ \\ldots,\\ |v_N| \\right).\n$$\n\nIt simply picks out the largest absolute value among the components of $\\mathbf{v}$.\n\nUsing this norm, we define the **Chebyshev distance** between two points $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ as\n\n$$\nd_\\infty(\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}) = \\max\\left( |a_1 - b_1|,\\ |a_2 - b_2|,\\ \\ldots,\\ |a_N - b_N| \\right).\n$$\n\nThis distance reflects the largest difference across coordinates. It’s especially useful in situations where we care about the worst-case difference in any direction.\n\nLet’s work through a few examples to see how this plays out in practice." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157518 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073740 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c42ab941-f231-5a97-a430-88c5273648a5" "question-f0e3ac9f-3533-517a-898b-d1cc891d8b27" "question-210d98b1-d520-5974-b07e-4d5e5c06b223"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c42ab941-f231-5a97-a430-88c5273648a5" :question/id #uuid "c42ab941-f231-5a97-a430-88c5273648a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-59898" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec8c3585db1981d001864b3c135f84e46643051d22f28e77f82e8b5a92d0f09d.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of squares to circles is $\\boxed{}:1$ because there are $\\boxed{}$ squares for every $1$ circle." :question/worked-solution "First, we count the number of squares. We see that there are $3$ squares.\n\nThen, we count the number of circles. We see that there are $3$ circles.\n\nSo the ratio of squares to circles is $3:3.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $3:3.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of squares to circles is $1:1$ because there is $1$ square for every $1$ circle.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e0e97fe2-4795-50cd-a7b5-3d08d498d02e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e0e97fe2-4795-50cd-a7b5-3d08d498d02e" :answer-field/id #uuid "e0e97fe2-4795-50cd-a7b5-3d08d498d02e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-45301129-1430-561c-975d-43df3220cccc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-45301129-1430-561c-975d-43df3220cccc" "answer-6c2a4ee8-1023-57d3-b19d-01bf628ad784" "answer-a5e6ce96-e241-5d81-839a-77f0c738feb0" "answer-a9f46437-76c7-59da-bffa-d2b285df56bb" "answer-d4c4007a-d8a2-5dec-b9fd-1ec8181c9d5a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45301129-1430-561c-975d-43df3220cccc" :answer/id #uuid "45301129-1430-561c-975d-43df3220cccc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c2a4ee8-1023-57d3-b19d-01bf628ad784" :answer/id #uuid "6c2a4ee8-1023-57d3-b19d-01bf628ad784" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5e6ce96-e241-5d81-839a-77f0c738feb0" :answer/id #uuid "a5e6ce96-e241-5d81-839a-77f0c738feb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9f46437-76c7-59da-bffa-d2b285df56bb" :answer/id #uuid "a9f46437-76c7-59da-bffa-d2b285df56bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4c4007a-d8a2-5dec-b9fd-1ec8181c9d5a" :answer/id #uuid "d4c4007a-d8a2-5dec-b9fd-1ec8181c9d5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f0e3ac9f-3533-517a-898b-d1cc891d8b27" :question/id #uuid "f0e3ac9f-3533-517a-898b-d1cc891d8b27" :question/math-academy-id "q-59903" :question/problem "There are $15$ girls and $9$ boys in a geometry class. What number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of girls to boys is $5:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $5$ girls for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ boys." :question/worked-solution "Since there are $15$ girls and $9$ boys, the ratio of girls to boys is $15:9.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,15 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,5 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $5:3$ is equivalent to $15:9.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of girls to boys is $5:3\\,$ because there are $5$ girls for every $3\\,$ boys.\"\n\nHence, the missing number is $3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f8f42b0d-1f1e-5535-971e-bc06dfe092ed"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f8f42b0d-1f1e-5535-971e-bc06dfe092ed" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f42b0d-1f1e-5535-971e-bc06dfe092ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a6de607f-d322-5729-a80a-a71d1e3f5ccd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d32b53af-b5dd-51b9-bb5d-778d1da7078c" "answer-be92aa79-3698-5510-9935-1b8212508906" "answer-a6de607f-d322-5729-a80a-a71d1e3f5ccd" "answer-d7ab97c6-7fa2-5c7d-b127-0ede64e61ee3" "answer-4100d4ff-ed37-52a8-9450-a287631a77bd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d32b53af-b5dd-51b9-bb5d-778d1da7078c" :answer/id #uuid "d32b53af-b5dd-51b9-bb5d-778d1da7078c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be92aa79-3698-5510-9935-1b8212508906" :answer/id #uuid "be92aa79-3698-5510-9935-1b8212508906" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6de607f-d322-5729-a80a-a71d1e3f5ccd" :answer/id #uuid "a6de607f-d322-5729-a80a-a71d1e3f5ccd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7ab97c6-7fa2-5c7d-b127-0ede64e61ee3" :answer/id #uuid "d7ab97c6-7fa2-5c7d-b127-0ede64e61ee3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4100d4ff-ed37-52a8-9450-a287631a77bd" :answer/id #uuid "4100d4ff-ed37-52a8-9450-a287631a77bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-210d98b1-d520-5974-b07e-4d5e5c06b223" :question/id #uuid "210d98b1-d520-5974-b07e-4d5e5c06b223" :question/math-academy-id "q-59905" :question/problem "There are $8$ students with blonde hair and $12$ students with black hair in a ballet class. What number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $2:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $2$ students with blonde hair for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ students with black hair." :question/worked-solution "Since there are $8$ students with blonde hair and $12$ students with black hair, the ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $8:12.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,8 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,12 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,2 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $2:3$ is equivalent to $8:12.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $2:3\\,$ because there are $2$ students with blonde hair for every $3\\,$ students with black hair.\"\n\nHence, the missing number is $3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2089b399-51c9-5b3c-8aca-f12d0c324c24"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2089b399-51c9-5b3c-8aca-f12d0c324c24" :answer-field/id #uuid "2089b399-51c9-5b3c-8aca-f12d0c324c24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c0d821f5-db5e-51a5-910e-5dfafa63518c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d52ce07d-1be8-57ce-8afa-0dbaac7ec0bf" "answer-c0d821f5-db5e-51a5-910e-5dfafa63518c" "answer-ccf427e4-c3bd-59be-8e07-eb80d2971207" "answer-3329434f-8065-5d0b-a666-f563fb8143ba" "answer-7fa0127b-9b67-546b-b18b-95b43306edd3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d52ce07d-1be8-57ce-8afa-0dbaac7ec0bf" :answer/id #uuid "d52ce07d-1be8-57ce-8afa-0dbaac7ec0bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0d821f5-db5e-51a5-910e-5dfafa63518c" :answer/id #uuid "c0d821f5-db5e-51a5-910e-5dfafa63518c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccf427e4-c3bd-59be-8e07-eb80d2971207" :answer/id #uuid "ccf427e4-c3bd-59be-8e07-eb80d2971207" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3329434f-8065-5d0b-a666-f563fb8143ba" :answer/id #uuid "3329434f-8065-5d0b-a666-f563fb8143ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fa0127b-9b67-546b-b18b-95b43306edd3" :answer/id #uuid "7fa0127b-9b67-546b-b18b-95b43306edd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-24f1cf90-b2d9-5450-9055-e10683bb7bac" :activity/id #uuid "24f1cf90-b2d9-5450-9055-e10683bb7bac" :activity/title "Equivalent Ratios" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048508 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-b1a71eab-7b52-5146-8e98-812bf70a532c" "step-e6ad2332-1f0b-5002-894a-06b6c20121c3" "step-27569124-56eb-5998-a405-7cf8748dd2b9"] :activity/first-step "step-b1a71eab-7b52-5146-8e98-812bf70a532c" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-b1a71eab-7b52-5146-8e98-812bf70a532c" :step/id #uuid "b1a71eab-7b52-5146-8e98-812bf70a532c" :step/content "question-c42ab941-f231-5a97-a430-88c5273648a5" :step/next "step-e6ad2332-1f0b-5002-894a-06b6c20121c3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e6ad2332-1f0b-5002-894a-06b6c20121c3" :step/id #uuid "e6ad2332-1f0b-5002-894a-06b6c20121c3" :step/content "question-f0e3ac9f-3533-517a-898b-d1cc891d8b27" :step/next "step-27569124-56eb-5998-a405-7cf8748dd2b9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-27569124-56eb-5998-a405-7cf8748dd2b9" :step/id #uuid "27569124-56eb-5998-a405-7cf8748dd2b9" :step/content "question-210d98b1-d520-5974-b07e-4d5e5c06b223" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100852 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4f9b4143-02aa-5852-84a2-99409167bad1" "question-35ae1f33-1dce-5ef7-af9a-1cf242b3dd81"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f9b4143-02aa-5852-84a2-99409167bad1" :question/id #uuid "4f9b4143-02aa-5852-84a2-99409167bad1" :question/math-academy-id "q-131247" :question/problem "Find the value of $f(3).$\n\n$f(3)\\,=\\,$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(3)-f(-3) \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(3)-(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}) \\\\ f(3) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe definite integral of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ over $[-3,3]$ is the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis. The corresponding region is a semicircle, so we find the area using the formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=A=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+\\frac{9\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-27491965-a7e7-5720-b84e-bc2ff10d5755"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-27491965-a7e7-5720-b84e-bc2ff10d5755" :answer-field/id #uuid "27491965-a7e7-5720-b84e-bc2ff10d5755" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-830e9698-d539-5352-8d7d-febdec432ecb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-830e9698-d539-5352-8d7d-febdec432ecb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-830e9698-d539-5352-8d7d-febdec432ecb" :answer/id #uuid "830e9698-d539-5352-8d7d-febdec432ecb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-35ae1f33-1dce-5ef7-af9a-1cf242b3dd81" :question/id #uuid "35ae1f33-1dce-5ef7-af9a-1cf242b3dd81" :question/math-academy-id "q-131248" :question/problem "$f(6)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(6)-f(-3) \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(6)-(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}) \\\\ f(6) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ on $[-3,6]$ consists of two parts: \n\n* an arc of a quarter circle for $x\\in [-3,3],$ which lies above the $x$-axis, and \n\n* a line segment for $x\\in [3,6],$ which lies below the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe first integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,3].$ Earlier, we found that\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe second integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,6].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ab \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the original integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find $f(6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-6) \\\\  & =2\\pi -6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b7e79ac7-4b47-5738-a30f-3fb1606b45fc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b7e79ac7-4b47-5738-a30f-3fb1606b45fc" :answer-field/id #uuid "b7e79ac7-4b47-5738-a30f-3fb1606b45fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b1fab290-16e7-5979-bd17-fff2703816f1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b1fab290-16e7-5979-bd17-fff2703816f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1fab290-16e7-5979-bd17-fff2703816f1" :answer/id #uuid "b1fab290-16e7-5979-bd17-fff2703816f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078406 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-168e3b6f-08dd-5743-a200-729abea32e10"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-168e3b6f-08dd-5743-a200-729abea32e10" :question/id #uuid "168e3b6f-08dd-5743-a200-729abea32e10" :question/math-academy-id "q-131249" :question/problem "Find all the values of $x$ in the interval $(-6,6)$ where the function $f(x)$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$f(x)$ is strictly increasing for $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\,,\\,$  \n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n* If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then $f(x)$ is strictly increasing on $(a,b).$\n\n* If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on $(a,b).$\n\nThe graph below shows that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is positive on the interval $(-3,3).$\n\n![](images/c7/c72ad85a6998493f29289074a4750f2830e8fdefe51d6f81536257abfb8ae008.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly increasing when $x\\in (-3,3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6bdc8cd7-5cdf-51e5-b082-29fd4628e097" "answer-field-34a9d8b0-3ced-5c46-80bb-9db0e68239b5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6bdc8cd7-5cdf-51e5-b082-29fd4628e097" :answer-field/id #uuid "6bdc8cd7-5cdf-51e5-b082-29fd4628e097" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-746f9f87-2e01-5d82-b120-0a1a9057b358" :answer-field/choices ["answer-746f9f87-2e01-5d82-b120-0a1a9057b358"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-746f9f87-2e01-5d82-b120-0a1a9057b358" :answer/id #uuid "746f9f87-2e01-5d82-b120-0a1a9057b358" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-34a9d8b0-3ced-5c46-80bb-9db0e68239b5" :answer-field/id #uuid "34a9d8b0-3ced-5c46-80bb-9db0e68239b5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4db1f1a3-190a-5cc1-93b3-d9f877fdb7e2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4db1f1a3-190a-5cc1-93b3-d9f877fdb7e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4db1f1a3-190a-5cc1-93b3-d9f877fdb7e2" :answer/id #uuid "4db1f1a3-190a-5cc1-93b3-d9f877fdb7e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078414 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e8c45a53-973c-5ec6-9492-ec10ad530490"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e8c45a53-973c-5ec6-9492-ec10ad530490" :question/id #uuid "e8c45a53-973c-5ec6-9492-ec10ad530490" :question/math-academy-id "q-131250" :question/problem "Find the value of $x\\in (-6,6)$ where the function $f(x)$ has a relative maximum.\n\nThe relative maximum is at $x\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=a$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n* ${f}^{′}(a)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n* ${f}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign from positive to negative (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-6,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,6)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=3$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d2db4504-8093-5779-84d9-e20e844db65c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d2db4504-8093-5779-84d9-e20e844db65c" :answer-field/id #uuid "d2db4504-8093-5779-84d9-e20e844db65c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-099bbe07-6cae-5cae-80ad-72300adb64c7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-099bbe07-6cae-5cae-80ad-72300adb64c7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-099bbe07-6cae-5cae-80ad-72300adb64c7" :answer/id #uuid "099bbe07-6cae-5cae-80ad-72300adb64c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100863 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7f985c78-b1b4-50b7-b3f8-18827e16fba5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7f985c78-b1b4-50b7-b3f8-18827e16fba5" :question/id #uuid "7f985c78-b1b4-50b7-b3f8-18827e16fba5" :question/math-academy-id "q-131251" :question/problem "Given that $f(-3)=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2},$ find the absolute minimum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,6].$\n\n${x}_{\\text{min}}\\,=\\,$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\,f({x}_{\\text{min}})\\,=\\,$  \n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The minimum value of the function $f(x)$ on $[-6,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\nTo find the minimum value, we apply three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points. From the graph, we see that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ and the endpoints of the interval are $x=-6,6.$ So, the candidates are $x=-6,\\,-3,\\,3,\\,6.$ \n\n**Step 2**: Discard any candidates that could not be a minimum.\n\n* The derivative is negative on $(-6,-3),$ so $f(x)$ is decreasing on that interval, and therefore $f(-6)>f(-3).$ So, $x=-6$ cannot be a minimum. \n\n* The derivative is positive on $(-3,3),$ so $f(x)$ is increasing on that interval, and therefore $f(-3)<f(3).$ So, $x=3$ cannot be a minimum. \n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the values of $f(x)$ at the remaining candidates. We have discarded $x=-6,\\,3,$ so the remaining candidates are $x=-3,\\,6.$\n\n* We are given that $f(-3)=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$\n\n* Previously, we found that $f(6)=2\\pi -6.$\n\nSo, comparing $f(-3)=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}≈-7.85$ and $f(6)=2\\pi -6≈0.28,$ we conclude that the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,6]$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2},$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5e655be1-21b1-5727-9f22-40f30bd8c752" "answer-field-ae9709fc-b41f-5d8a-b57e-49646ab638ff"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5e655be1-21b1-5727-9f22-40f30bd8c752" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e655be1-21b1-5727-9f22-40f30bd8c752" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c6fa3df3-dbe3-5475-acbd-66da493953b8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c6fa3df3-dbe3-5475-acbd-66da493953b8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6fa3df3-dbe3-5475-acbd-66da493953b8" :answer/id #uuid "c6fa3df3-dbe3-5475-acbd-66da493953b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ae9709fc-b41f-5d8a-b57e-49646ab638ff" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae9709fc-b41f-5d8a-b57e-49646ab638ff" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b3b04dd9-266e-5d29-a13a-aa5b92aacd6f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b3b04dd9-266e-5d29-a13a-aa5b92aacd6f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3b04dd9-266e-5d29-a13a-aa5b92aacd6f" :answer/id #uuid "b3b04dd9-266e-5d29-a13a-aa5b92aacd6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068893 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5064a9ef-4f8e-5fbe-8d7d-2164ebe22645"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-5064a9ef-4f8e-5fbe-8d7d-2164ebe22645" :question/id #uuid "5064a9ef-4f8e-5fbe-8d7d-2164ebe22645" :question/math-academy-id "q-131252" :question/problem "${f}^{″}(4)=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=({f}^{′}(x))^{′}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that $4\\in [3,6],$ where the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is represented by a straight line segment. So, ${f}^{″}(4)$ is just the slope of this segment.\n\nSince this segment passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,0)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(6,-4),$ we can compute its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{-4-0}{6-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{″}(4)=-\\frac{4}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e63b5122-3053-56d2-9da4-5e85f3949a91"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e63b5122-3053-56d2-9da4-5e85f3949a91" :answer-field/id #uuid "e63b5122-3053-56d2-9da4-5e85f3949a91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-bc78b909-0880-5440-888c-d28056945b8b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bc78b909-0880-5440-888c-d28056945b8b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc78b909-0880-5440-888c-d28056945b8b" :answer/id #uuid "bc78b909-0880-5440-888c-d28056945b8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166865 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0343bcd3-e974-592d-9f5b-2241e197ca26"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0343bcd3-e974-592d-9f5b-2241e197ca26" :question/id #uuid "0343bcd3-e974-592d-9f5b-2241e197ca26" :question/math-academy-id "q-131253" :question/problem "Given that the point $(2,\\sqrt{5})$ lies on the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ find ${f}^{″}(2).$\n\n${f}^{″}(2)\\,=\\,$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({f}^{′}(x))\n$$\n\nis the slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$ \n\nNotice that the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ on $x\\in [-3,3]$ is a semicircle of a radius $3$ centered at $(0,0).$ Its equation (for $y>0$) is given by\n\n$$\n(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}={3}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(2,\\,\\sqrt{5})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-87ca15d6-48a6-50a1-9d31-763d81685807"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-87ca15d6-48a6-50a1-9d31-763d81685807" :answer-field/id #uuid "87ca15d6-48a6-50a1-9d31-763d81685807" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7532c1eb-1150-5b3d-abb1-342bec35e705" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7532c1eb-1150-5b3d-abb1-342bec35e705"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7532c1eb-1150-5b3d-abb1-342bec35e705" :answer/id #uuid "7532c1eb-1150-5b3d-abb1-342bec35e705" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175947 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-51f72acf-5b7c-5f3d-bea9-1b31e5199996"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-51f72acf-5b7c-5f3d-bea9-1b31e5199996" :question/id #uuid "51f72acf-5b7c-5f3d-bea9-1b31e5199996" :question/math-academy-id "q-131254" :question/problem "How many inflection points does the function $f(x)$ have inside the interval $(-6,6),$ and what is the smallest value of $x$ where $f(x)$ has an inflection point?\n\nThere are {{field-1}} \ninflection points in total.\n\nThe inflection point with the smallest $x$-coordinate is at\n\n$x\\,=\\,$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-6,6].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of ${f}^{″}(x).$ Recall the following:\n\n* When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$  \n\n* When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ \n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n* ${f}^{″}(a)=0$ or ${f}^{″}(a)$ is undefined, and \n\n* ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nSo, we summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-6,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,6)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the table, we see that ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign only at the point $x=0.$  Therefore, $x=0$  is a point of inflection.\n\nFinally, the smallest value of $x$ inside the interval $(-6,6),$ where the function $f(x)$ has an inflection point is $x=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f11fbe67-fc53-5d48-89e6-804af1e42c3a" "answer-field-f041b7b3-189c-536b-a1e4-71dd95312f8d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f11fbe67-fc53-5d48-89e6-804af1e42c3a" :answer-field/id #uuid "f11fbe67-fc53-5d48-89e6-804af1e42c3a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3c7380a8-a7e4-58c2-b50a-6db0fdc55270" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3c7380a8-a7e4-58c2-b50a-6db0fdc55270"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c7380a8-a7e4-58c2-b50a-6db0fdc55270" :answer/id #uuid "3c7380a8-a7e4-58c2-b50a-6db0fdc55270" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f041b7b3-189c-536b-a1e4-71dd95312f8d" :answer-field/id #uuid "f041b7b3-189c-536b-a1e4-71dd95312f8d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-93061708-c840-5745-b2ec-76afc08011b1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-93061708-c840-5745-b2ec-76afc08011b1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93061708-c840-5745-b2ec-76afc08011b1" :answer/id #uuid "93061708-c840-5745-b2ec-76afc08011b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "multistep-33ad15c6-26fa-53bb-a622-360a616b1dba" :multistep/id #uuid "33ad15c6-26fa-53bb-a622-360a616b1dba" :multistep/context "![](images/75/75f62c88d82d57bb43d69db5b62afd0f990b0a148c5d22cb48b2cfefe7f331aa.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,6].$ It's known that $f(-3)=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ consists of two line segments and a semicircle, as shown above." :multistep/steps ["step-9a86e554-c5c9-5718-9489-c117a9627702" "step-69491b2a-a5ed-5fa6-a221-d175e24ec4e0" "step-def1c002-216c-5001-b8e9-ea2d30f58499" "step-b4d0740f-ad2a-58b0-8909-347552dcb60c" "step-5134eb47-1b80-50ad-b424-7660492a9a51" "step-3f9db76f-8817-5cc7-bb78-f7980da5c892" "step-e04287ab-4d9a-50f5-98eb-fef6af282e29" "step-cfdb0a23-6450-58bd-9871-9efb860fbf90"] :multistep/first-step "step-9a86e554-c5c9-5718-9489-c117a9627702" :db/ensure [:multistep/validate]} {:db/id "step-9a86e554-c5c9-5718-9489-c117a9627702" :step/id #uuid "9a86e554-c5c9-5718-9489-c117a9627702" :step/content "question-4f9b4143-02aa-5852-84a2-99409167bad1" :step/next "step-69491b2a-a5ed-5fa6-a221-d175e24ec4e0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-69491b2a-a5ed-5fa6-a221-d175e24ec4e0" :step/id #uuid "69491b2a-a5ed-5fa6-a221-d175e24ec4e0" :step/content "question-35ae1f33-1dce-5ef7-af9a-1cf242b3dd81" :step/next "step-def1c002-216c-5001-b8e9-ea2d30f58499" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-def1c002-216c-5001-b8e9-ea2d30f58499" :step/id #uuid "def1c002-216c-5001-b8e9-ea2d30f58499" :step/content "question-168e3b6f-08dd-5743-a200-729abea32e10" :step/next "step-b4d0740f-ad2a-58b0-8909-347552dcb60c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b4d0740f-ad2a-58b0-8909-347552dcb60c" :step/id #uuid "b4d0740f-ad2a-58b0-8909-347552dcb60c" :step/content "question-e8c45a53-973c-5ec6-9492-ec10ad530490" :step/next "step-5134eb47-1b80-50ad-b424-7660492a9a51" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5134eb47-1b80-50ad-b424-7660492a9a51" :step/id #uuid "5134eb47-1b80-50ad-b424-7660492a9a51" :step/content "question-7f985c78-b1b4-5